<div class="page-header"><div class="page-header-inner">
  <div class="eyebrow">// Program Chronology</div>
  <h1 class="sh">Development Timeline</h1>
  <p class="ss">70 years of pressure garment evolution — US and Soviet programs in parallel</p>
</div></div>
<section class="section">
  <div class="timeline-cols">
    <div>
      <div class="eyebrow c" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">— United States</div>
      <div class="timeline-wrap">
        <div class="tl-item tl-us"><div class="tl-year c">1917–1936</div><div class="tl-program">Pre-space pressure-suit ancestry</div><div class="tl-desc">Early high-altitude pressure suit concepts establish the core problem: pressure enclosure without losing human function.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-us"><div class="tl-year c">1959–1963</div><div class="tl-program">Mercury IVA · B.F. Goodrich</div><div class="tl-desc">First US manned spacesuit. 22 lb, vehicle-fed, 3.7 psi. Flotation deficiency exposed after hatch jettison. Water survival lessons drove all future IVA design.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-us"><div class="tl-year c">1965–1966</div><div class="tl-program">Gemini G4C / ELSS · David Clark</div><div class="tl-desc">First US EVA learning loop. Gemini IV exhaustion proved EVA operations, body restraint and glove productivity are co-dependent. Progressive iteration across 10 missions.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-us"><div class="tl-year c">1968–1975</div><div class="tl-program">Apollo A7L · A7LB + PLSS</div><div class="tl-desc">Autonomous PLSS and true lunar work suit. ILC + Hamilton Standard. Set benchmark for portable EVA autonomy. A7LB added 7-hr, 212 lb capability for J-missions and rover traverses.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-us"><div class="tl-year c">1973–1974</div><div class="tl-program">Skylab ALSA — umbilical EVA</div><div class="tl-desc">Station repair EVA returned to umbilical architecture. Showed station EVA can justify different life-support architecture from lunar EVA.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-us"><div class="tl-year c">1983–2020s</div><div class="tl-program">Shuttle EMU → Enhanced EMU</div><div class="tl-desc">Modular hard-upper-torso orbital EVA. Large productivity jump. EVA-23 water intrusion near-miss (2013). Aging fleet now on critical sustainment watch — OIG 2025.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-us"><div class="tl-year c">2022–</div><div class="tl-program">AxEMU · Axiom Space · Artemis</div><div class="tl-desc">Commercial lunar suit for Artemis III south-pole EVA. Direct embodiment of 60 years of failure lessons. Currently in qualification phase.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="eyebrow gold" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">— Soviet / Russian</div>
      <div class="timeline-wrap">
        <div class="tl-item tl-ru"><div class="tl-year gold">1931–1940</div><div class="tl-program">Soviet pre-space era · Chertovsky / TsAGI</div><div class="tl-desc">High-altitude suit work established the mobility/pressure challenge before spaceflight existed. The problem statement was Russian before it was American.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-ru"><div class="tl-year gold">1952 / 1961</div><div class="tl-program">Zvezda founded · SK-1 Vostok</div><div class="tl-desc">Plant No. 918 created. SK-1 flew on Gagarin's Vostok. 300 hPa, maskless ventilation, automatic visor closure, integrated ejection logic. SK-2 adapted for Tereshkova.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-ru"><div class="tl-year gold">1965</div><div class="tl-program">BERKUT · Voskhod-2 · First EVA</div><div class="tl-desc">Leonov's 12-minute spacewalk. Suit ballooning nearly prevented re-entry. Leonov had to partially deflate to re-enter airlock. Ingress became Requirement 1 for all future EVA design worldwide.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-ru"><div class="tl-year gold">1967–1972</div><div class="tl-program">KRECHET · Soviet Lunar Program</div><div class="tl-desc">Semi-rigid rear-entry lunar suit. Cancelled before flight. Rear-entry concept became Orlan DNA. The most important unflown suit in history.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-ru"><div class="tl-year gold">1973–present</div><div class="tl-program">SOKOL-K family · Soyuz rescue</div><div class="tl-desc">Long-lived Soyuz IVA rescue standard. Difficult donning, poor visibility, under-knee discomfort. Upgrade latency documented as primary failure mode.</div></div>
        <div class="tl-item tl-ru"><div class="tl-year gold">1977–present</div><div class="tl-program">Orlan-D → Orlan-MK · ISS EVA</div><div class="tl-desc">First operational Orlan on Salyut-6. 5.8 psi, 238 lb, 7-hour EVA. Fleet operations shifted challenge from design to maintenance, drying, recharge and humidity control.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
